@startuml client_connection_process_input_event_activity
title ClientConnection - processing received input

start
:ProcessInputEvent();
:ReceiveProtocolMessage(client_fd_, code);
if (Receive failed?) then (yes)
    if (errno == EAGAIN?) then (yes)
        :Log a spurious wake-up;
        :Return kNone;
        stop
    else (no)
        :Receive failure is not a spurious wake-up;
        if (errno == EPIPE?) then (yes)
            :Return kClosedByPeer;
        else (no)
            :Return kIoError;
        endif
        stop
    endif
else (no)
    :Use the received message span;
endif

if (code == REPLY?) then (yes)
    :Lock send_mutex_;
    if (waiting_for_reply_ is engaged?) then (yes)
        :Move its callback into a local ReplyCallback;
        :Reset waiting_for_reply_;
        :ProcessSendQueueUnderLock(lock);
        :Unlock send_mutex_;
        :Invoke the local callback with the received span;
        :Return kNone;
        stop
    else (no)
        :Unlock send_mutex_;
        :Return kIoError: reply with no occupied slot;
        stop
    endif
else (no)
    :The code is not REPLY;
    if (code == NOTIFY?) then (yes)
        if (notify_callback_ is non-empty?) then (yes)
            :Invoke notify_callback_ with the received span;
        else (no)
            :Ignore this notification;
        endif
    else (no)
        :Return kIoError: unrecognized message code;
        stop
    endif
endif

:Return kNone;
stop

legend
    Returned stop reasons are consumed by the endpoint input callback.
    This procedure does not itself claim stopping or unregister the endpoint.
    Reply processing drains queued sends before invoking the completed callback.
    Queue dispatch can therefore replace the free slot with a new pending reply.
    The reply guard checks optional engagement, not callback non-emptiness.
    An empty drain marker passes that guard; invocation has no empty check here.
    Notifications are ignored when no callback is installed, without stopping.
    Receive-size enforcement belongs to the engine; caller reply-buffer bounds
    are handled by the SendWaitReply completion callback.
endlegend

@enduml